%==================== 1. INTRODUCTION ====================
\chapter{Introduction}

\section{Purpose}
This document specifies the software requirements for the \textbf{Smart Airport Management System (SAMS)}, Version 1.0. SAMS is a web application that centralizes selected airport operational records and coordinates follow-up work between authorized airport departments.

SAMS builds on an existing working prototype, which gives a single administrator a central interface for flights, luggage, visitors, staff and transportation. This specification extends the prototype into a \emph{role- and department-based} system: each department works in its own workspace on the records it owns, other departments see the information they need for coordination, and significant operational events automatically notify the right people, create follow-up tasks and leave an accountable history.

This SRS is a requirements specification. It does not assert that the newly described features are already implemented; the status of every requirement is stated explicitly.

\section{Intended Audience and Reading Suggestions}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Project reviewers and stakeholders} --- to assess scope, feasibility and verifiability. Chapters 1 and 2 give the complete picture.
    \item \textbf{Development team (Group 5)} --- to plan and implement the system. Chapter 3 (System Features) and Chapter 5 (data, interfaces and phases) are the most relevant.
    \item \textbf{Testers} --- to derive test cases from requirement IDs (Section~\ref{sec:verification}).
    \item \textbf{Future maintainers} --- to understand roles, departments, data ownership and extension points.
\end{itemize}
Readers new to the project should start with the one-line summary below, then read Section~\ref{sec:users} (users and departments) and Section~\ref{sec:workflow} (example workflow).



\section{Project Scope}
Airport operations involve many departments --- flight operations, baggage handling, ground transport, maintenance and passenger services --- that depend on each other's information. When each department keeps isolated records, a problem in one area (for example, a baggage issue on a flight) is communicated late or inconsistently, nobody is clearly responsible for the follow-up, and there is no reliable record of who did what and when.

SAMS addresses this with one web application and one shared backend and database. Public flight and transport information is available without login. Passengers may optionally create an account, register their journeys and receive targeted notifications. Staff and administrators sign in through a common entry point and are redirected to the workspace appropriate to their role and department.

\subsection*{Objectives}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Centralize flight, luggage/baggage, visitor, staff and transportation records, retaining the existing prototype modules where practical.
    \item Enforce server-side authorization by \textbf{role, department, resource scope and permitted fields}.
    \item Provide separate role-aware workspaces within \textbf{one} React application, not separate codebases or login systems.
    \item Link operational events to \textbf{targeted departmental notifications and action items}.
    \item Provide an \textbf{incident and audit timeline} showing important changes, decisions, acknowledgements and outcomes.
    \item \textbf{Escalate overdue} high-priority tasks to the responsible supervisor under configurable rules.
    \item Provide passenger-specific journey and baggage notifications \textbf{without exposing another passenger's private information}.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection*{In Scope (initial expanded MVP)}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Existing authentication and management modules from the prototype, preserved and extended.
    \item Public, read-only flight, announcement and transport information.
    \item Passenger accounts, registered journeys and targeted in-app notifications, using demonstration data.
    \item Fully implemented workspaces for the first three departments: \textbf{Flight Operations, Baggage Handling and Ground Transport}.
    \item \textbf{Maintenance and Passenger Services} represented in the design and added as time permits (phased).
    \item Central administrator workspace, supervisor permissions, approval workflow, event processing, action-item tracking, audit timeline and overdue-task escalation.
    \item Testing with seeded demonstration records and one complete cross-department incident scenario.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Out of Scope (initial expanded MVP)}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Live airline, airport, RFID, air-traffic or baggage-scanner integrations.
    \item Real ticket booking, payments, check-in or boarding-pass issuance.
    \item Real airline operational decisions such as guaranteed connection rebooking.
    \item SMS delivery and production notification providers (email may be a later extension).
    \item AI-based prediction, microservices and large-scale production deployment.
\end{itemize}

