\section{Design and Implementation Constraints}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Technology stack:} the prototype's technology stack is retained (React, Vite, React Router, Axios, Tailwind CSS, Recharts; Node.js, Express, Mongoose, JWT, bcryptjs; MongoDB; Git/GitHub) unless a blocker is found.
    \item \textbf{Architecture:} a modular monolith with one frontend, one API and one database --- not microservices and not separate applications per department.
    \item \textbf{Preserve working code:} existing working login and CRUD features shall be preserved and extended, not needlessly replaced.
    \item \textbf{Data:} no live airport or airline feed is assumed; demonstrations use seeded or manually entered data.
    \item \textbf{Secrets:} database credentials and JWT secrets are held in environment variables and are never committed to the repository.
    \item \textbf{Notifications:} in-app only for the MVP; no SMS or external delivery providers.
    \item \textbf{Team and schedule:} a four-member development team working to a fixed delivery schedule; Maintenance and Passenger Services are therefore phased.
    \item \textbf{Cost:} only free and open-source tools and free-tier hosting.
\end{itemize}

\section{Example Workflow: A Baggage Issue}
\label{sec:workflow}
The following scenario is the reference cross-department workflow for the MVP and the basis of the end-to-end acceptance test. Figure~\ref{fig:sequence} shows it as a UML sequence diagram.
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Baggage Handling staff report a baggage issue and link it to a flight.
    \item SAMS stores the issue and records an operational event, in the same transaction where supported.
    \item Impact rules identify the relevant recipients, such as Baggage Handling and Flight Operations.
    \item Relevant people receive in-app notifications, and follow-up action items are created.
    \item Flight Operations assesses any flight impact. A baggage report does \emph{not} automatically change the flight schedule.
    \item If a sensitive change is needed (e.g.\ a delay), it follows the approval policy and the decision is recorded.
    \item Only relevant passengers receive personal alerts; public pages show approved public information only.
    \item Staff record follow-up and resolution; the incident timeline preserves the full history.
\end{enumerate}

