NFR-11 & Production deployment shall use HTTPS and restrict cross-origin requests to the frontend origin. & Medium & Planned \\
\end{reqtable}

\noindent\textbf{Security and privacy rules}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Public registration must never grant a privileged role.
    \item Department and resource authorization is checked on the server for every relevant request.
    \item Passenger records and notifications are scoped to the authenticated passenger.
    \item Public endpoints return allowlisted public fields only.
    \item Sensitive changes follow the approval workflow.
    \item Audit data is access-controlled and cannot be edited by ordinary users.
    \item Events and notifications carry references and minimal display fields, not unnecessary personal data.
\end{itemize}

\section{Software Quality Attributes}
\begin{reqtable}
NFR-12 & \textbf{Usability:} the interface shall be responsive and usable on modern desktop and mobile browsers, with consistent forms, tables and readable error messages. & Medium & Implemented \\
NFR-13 & \textbf{Maintainability:} the code shall keep a modular separation of routes, middleware, controllers/services and models, and of frontend pages and components. & Medium & Implemented \\
NFR-14 & \textbf{Transparency:} the application shall clearly distinguish live database records from seeded demonstration data. & High & Planned \\
NFR-15 & \textbf{Extensibility:} a new department shall be addable through configuration (department, permissions, impact rules) and new pages, without changing the core authorization or event logic. & Medium & Planned \\
NFR-16 & \textbf{Testability:} every requirement shall be traceable to at least one test case (Section~\ref{sec:verification}). & Medium & Planned \\
\end{reqtable}

\section{Business Rules}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Every luggage, baggage-issue and transport record must reference an existing flight.
    \item A passenger can view only their own private journey and baggage details.
    \item A department may read relevant shared information without gaining write access to another department's records.
    \item A baggage report never changes the flight schedule automatically; Flight Operations decides.
    \item Notification acknowledgement does not equal task completion or incident resolution.
    \item A sensitive change remains pending until approved; the urgent path is available only when explicitly authorized and is always reviewed afterwards.
    \item A task is escalated only when its due time and rule are met; escalation stops when the task is resolved.
    \item A passenger alert must not state a missed connection or a confirmed alternative unless the data supports it.
\end{itemize}

